WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Elon just claimed that Tesla will ship a&nbsp;
fully functional humanoid robot by next year.
&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.200
Naturally the press thinks it’s&nbsp;
impossible. But could he actually do it?
&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.240
Let’s look at what’s possible now. Boston&nbsp;
Dynamics already has a robot that can do parkour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:20.400
and ten years ago they built a robot that could&nbsp;
run 28 miles per hour. The Tesla Bot will only go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.160
5 miles per hour, so it doesn’t seem impossible.
In fact, if Elon wanted to release a robot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.840
matched the capabilities of Boston Dynamics next&nbsp;
year, he could. And I’m being 100% serious.
&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.000
Boston Dynamics is only worth one&nbsp;
billion dollars, but Elon is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.040
over a hundred and eighty billion dollars, so he&nbsp;
could just buy Boston Dynamics, paint the Atlas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.240
robot black, and start producing them next year.
Obviously Elon isn’t going to do that, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.760
fact that he could should make you pause before&nbsp;
you say that Tesla Bot will not ship next year.
&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.960
And even if Elon misses his&nbsp;
target ship date by years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.920
does it really matter? Two things are clearly&nbsp;
true: building the Tesla bot doesn’t break the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.640
laws of physics, so it’s possible. And building&nbsp;
such an advanced piece of technology will require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.440
lots of smart people to work on the project, and&nbsp;
that’s why Tesla is recruiting so aggressively.
&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.760
Elon is known for always announcing&nbsp;
unrealistic timelines and then missing them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.560
sometimes by years. But I think this is a lot&nbsp;
less important than most people seem to think.
&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.680
Everyone agrees that Steve Jobs was one of the&nbsp;
most impactful technologists of the last hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.520
years. But he missed deadlines all the time too.
In 1983, he said he wanted to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.920
networked computer the size of a book and&nbsp;
would ship it by the end of the decade:
&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.080
“Apple’s strategy is really simple. What we&nbsp;
want to do is we want to put an incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.040
great computer in a book that you can&nbsp;
carry around with you and learn how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.200
use in 20 minutes. That’s what we want&nbsp;
to do and we want to do it this decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.160
And we really want to do it with a radio&nbsp;
link in it so you don’t have to hook up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.760
to anything and you’re in communication with all&nbsp;
of these larger databases and other computers.”
&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.160
Well, the first iPad didn’t ship until 2010, so&nbsp;
Jobs missed his prediction by a full 20 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.720
but it didn’t matter at all. In&nbsp;
the time between 1983 and 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.200
Apple shipped dozens of incredible products&nbsp;
that moved the entire industry forward.
&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.000
Looking back now, no one remembers that Jobs&nbsp;
missed that deadline! Just that when he finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.720
did ship the iPad, it was a great product.
That Steve Jobs clip comes from a talk he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.160
gave in Aspen and his goal with the talk was to&nbsp;
recruit talented engineers to come work for Apple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.200
Elon is doing the exact same thing with AI Day.&nbsp;
He’s highlighting all the incredible advances that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.440
Tesla has made in the field of self-driving&nbsp;
and teasing what is going to come next. 
&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.360
And it’s the advances in self-driving&nbsp;
that we should really be focusing on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.440
because the stuff that Tesla is doing&nbsp;
right now is absolutely incredible.
&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.360
The entire company has basically transformed over&nbsp;
the past two decades. What started as a hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.004
company focused on building cars has transformed&nbsp;
into a software company focused on building self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.004 --> 00:02:54.960
driving technology. And now, the AI models that&nbsp;
power Tesla’s self-driving cars have become so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.920
complex, that Tesla has gone a layer deeper and&nbsp;
become a hardware company that makes computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.160
chips. It’s really incredible stuff.
During Tesla’s AI day, Elon’s team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.400
explained how they are tackling hard&nbsp;
problems across three key domains.
&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.760
The first is the core neural network architecture&nbsp;
that powers autopilot. It’s a common misperception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.440
that self-driving cars use a single AI system&nbsp;
to process camera data and then control the car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.920
There are a few companies that are trying to&nbsp;
do that type of “end-to-end” self-driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.640
like Comma AI, but Tesla actually breaks&nbsp;
the problem down into smaller parts.
&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.560
AI is only used to understand&nbsp;
the environment around the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.280
the actual decision making about where to drive&nbsp;
within that environment is developed using more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.600
traditional software development techniques. 
Even though Tesla could use machine learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.040
to plan out the route taken by the car, they&nbsp;
don’t, and Elon had a great explanation for why:
&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.200
“As long as you’ve got surround video neural&nbsp;
nets to understand what’s going on and can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.600
convert those surround video into vector&nbsp;
space, then you basically have a video game. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.720
you’re like in Grand Theft Auto, you can make the&nbsp;
cars drive around without crashing. You don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.200
to have a neural net for control and planning."
See, programmers have been able to design basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.680
systems for cars in video games for years that&nbsp;
react to environments and avoid collisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.320
That part is actually not that hard. The&nbsp;
hard part is understanding the real world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.480
and all of it’s incredible variations&nbsp;
as if it was as simple as a video game.
&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.160
Elon mentioned the importance of “surround video”&nbsp;
and “vector space” because that’s where Tesla took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.000
a big step forward. Fortunately, those two phrases&nbsp;
sound more complicated than they actually are.
&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.520
Surround video just means stitching all of the&nbsp;
different cameras around the car into one big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.680
image. Years ago, when Tesla implemented the first&nbsp;
version of autopilot, the car only had one camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.920
so this wasn’t an issue. The only job of&nbsp;
the autopilot was to keep you in the lane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.800
and stop you from running into a car directly&nbsp;
in front of you. But now, Tesla cars ship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.360
lots of cameras placed all around the car. 
This creates a ton more data for the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.160
learning algorithm to process, and up until&nbsp;
recently, the Tesla systems were dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.600
with each camera feed individually.&nbsp;
This caused some problems though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.320
like when a big truck appeared on multiple&nbsp;
cameras, it became very difficult to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.920
exactly where that truck was in the real world.
Allowing the system to see all the cameras at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.265
once as “surround video” also made it possible to&nbsp;
translate the two dimensional images into three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.265 --> 00:05:28.560
dimensional representations. That’s what Elon is&nbsp;
talking about when he says “vector space”. The new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.400
autopilot system now takes all of the camera data&nbsp;
and creates a fully 3d representation of the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.800
just like a video game. And this has some&nbsp;
powerful implications, especially on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.000
second key Tesla innovation: training data.
Every machine learning system needs data and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.440
there are three main ways that companies generally&nbsp;
get data. The most reliable data is hand-labeled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.800
by a human, but that’s very expensive. Using&nbsp;
a separate system to automatically label data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.120
is much cheaper, but it can be prone to&nbsp;
error. Lastly, you can create an infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:07.360
amount of data using simulated environments.
AI experts have debated which kind of data is best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:13.600
for years, but Tesla just said “yes, and” to all&nbsp;
three kinds. They hired a thousand data labelers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.160
built an automated system for labeling the data&nbsp;
that the humans couldn’t get to in time, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.520
also developed a remarkably realistic simulation&nbsp;
environment for generating even more data.
&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.800
All of these systems help teach AutoPilot about&nbsp;
various situations it might see in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.120
And they all support each other and&nbsp;
help fill gaps in the data. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.320
the simulated environment is great for creating&nbsp;
bizarre scenarios like a group of people running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.040
down the middle of a highway. It’s unlikely to&nbsp;
happen in the real world, but if it does happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.520
you’ll want your Tesla to know what to do.
And all of this is aided by that move to “vector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.560
space” that Elon mentioned. Human labelers can&nbsp;
now manually identify a traffic cone in a 3D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.600
environment that looks like a video game and&nbsp;
the raw images from each camera that saw that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.920
traffic cone will be updated automatically.
This three pronged approach shows that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.600
Autopilot team isn’t turning away&nbsp;
any good ideas. They’ll use every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:10.960
tool at their disposal to build a reliable&nbsp;
system. But doing so creates new problems.
&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.680
Tesla now has hundreds of thousands of cars&nbsp;
sending in real world video feeds every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.880
day, and most of those videos get labeled&nbsp;
with either by humans or automated systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.720
so the AI team at Tesla now has an&nbsp;
immense amount of data to work with.
&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.880
They train their models multiple times per month,&nbsp;
which means running all of that data through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.920
massive supercomputer in order to recalculate&nbsp;
the entire neural network. This is an incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.720
computationally complex task and it has to&nbsp;
be done on a special type of computer chip.
&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.200
Traditionally, the training of neural networks&nbsp;
has been done using graphics processing units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.240
or GPUs, and there’s really only one major&nbsp;
player in the GPU space at the moment:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:55.280
Nvidia. The problem is that Nvidia has increased&nbsp;
their prices to the point where it no longer makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:59.840
sense for big companies like Tesla. 
But there is another option. In 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.520
Google released the Tensor Processing Unit,&nbsp;
or TPU. It’s a chip specifically designed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.280
training these machine learning models.&nbsp;
It’s a good option for many companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.440
but it wouldn’t work for Tesla because they&nbsp;
are in direct competition with Google.
&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.000
See, you can buy an Nvidia graphics card&nbsp;
and basically do whatever you want with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.880
But Google’s TPUs are kept behind lock&nbsp;
and key in Google’s Cloud datacenters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:28.000
and in order to use them, you have to agree to&nbsp;
their terms of service. The thing is, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.200
clauses in that agreement states that you promise&nbsp;
not to use their systems to train self-driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:39.120
cars. So that option is off the table for Tesla.
But Elon saw this coming and had the Tesla team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.400
start building their own “D1” chips that would&nbsp;
serve as the basis for a new supercomputer called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.520
Dojo that could be used to train autopilot. They&nbsp;
still haven’t deployed this system fully, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.080
anyone who was paying close attention, this was&nbsp;
by far the biggest announcement of the day. 
&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:59.040
The scale of everything they are doing is&nbsp;
absolutely insane. A single D1 chip is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.720
thousand times more powerful than a brand new PS5.&nbsp;
And Tesla has connected one million of these chips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.240
together to create their compute center. The&nbsp;
complete system has 1.1 exaflop of computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.040
power, which is so massive, it’s likely the&nbsp;
most powerful supercomputer ever created.
&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.560
The combination of improvements to&nbsp;
the neural network, training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:24.000
and underlying hardware are going to compound&nbsp;
to help Tesla finally get across the finish line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
and deliver full self-driving. But&nbsp;
Elon obviously isn’t stopping there.
&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:33.600
After over an hour of technical demonstrations,&nbsp;
Elon decided to have some fun and spent 5 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.680
talking about the Tesla bot. He’s clearly&nbsp;
having fun with it, but I do think that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.440
really believes that Tesla will be able&nbsp;
to transfer a lot of the work they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:46.480
done in self-driving to the humanoid form. 
But there’s a big question, why try to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.040
it look so much like a person? Science fiction&nbsp;
authors have been writing about humanoid robots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.840
for decades, but building something with all&nbsp;
the flexibility of the human body is incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:01.920
difficult. It’s much easier to build a robot&nbsp;
vacuum than a robot that vacuums after all.
&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.320
I think the humanoid form could actually wind&nbsp;
up being an intermediate step on the path to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.480
even more useful robots. Kind of like how&nbsp;
Apple tried to make everyone look analogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.560
in the first versions of the iPhone.&nbsp;
Consider the calculator app. In the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.280
running calculations on a computer required&nbsp;
keying in values on a command line, the device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.000
looked nothing like a real-world calculator.&nbsp;
But when Apple released the iPhone, they opted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.800
for a “skeuomorphic” design that mimicked&nbsp;
physical calculators. Only after a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.720
did Apple realize that users didn’t need this&nbsp;
familiarity, and opted for a more minimal design.
&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:40.640
The same could be true for robotics. We could&nbsp;
move from iRobot vacuum cleaners, to Tesla bots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:45.120
and then to something entirely new.&nbsp;
But I’m excited to see where it goes.
&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:51.600
And remember, even if Elon delivered the Tesla&nbsp;
bot in the year 2040, he would still be faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:59.520
at shipping than Steve Jobs, and that would&nbsp;
be pretty incredible. Thanks for watching.

